# 🌐 Networking Theory

# Address Resolution Protocol (ARP)

ARP stands for Address Resolution Protocol.

ARP maps an IPv4 address to its corresponding MAC address on the local network.

IP addresses operate at Layer 3 (Network Layer), while Ethernet communication requires MAC addresses at Layer 2 (Data Link Layer).

ARP connects these two layers.

---

# Why ARP is Needed

Suppose a computer wants to send data to:

```
192.168.1.1
```

The computer knows the destination IP address but cannot send Ethernet frames until it knows the destination MAC address.

ARP broadcasts a request asking:

> "Who has IP address 192.168.1.1?"

The destination device replies with its MAC address.

The computer stores this mapping temporarily in the ARP cache.

---

# ARP Flow

```
IP Address

↓

ARP Request

↓

MAC Address

↓

Ethernet Communication
```

---

# View ARP Cache

```bash
ip neigh
```

Displays IP-to-MAC address mappings stored on the system.

Example:

```text
192.168.1.1 dev eth0 lladdr aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff REACHABLE
```

